name: job control
boss: BackgroundNoise
flavor: I keep running after you look away.
brief: A & at the end runs a command in the background and hands you back the
brief: prompt. jobs lists what you started, fg brings one forward, bg resumes a
brief: stopped one. ^Z stops the job in front of you.
brief:
brief: $! is the pid of the last thing you backgrounded. wait blocks until it
brief: finishes. That pair is how a script starts several things at once and
brief: then collects them.
brief:
brief: All of this dies when the terminal does -- which is what abduco is for.
---
q: run "sleep 2" in the background
a: sleep 2 &
ref: sleep
teach: sleep
hint: an ampersand at the end
---
q: run "sleep 2" in the background and print the pid it was given
a: sleep 2 & echo $!
out: ^[0-9]+$
ref: sleep
teach: sleep
---
q: start "sleep 1" in the background and then wait for it to finish
a: sleep 1 & wait
ref: wait
teach: wait
---
q: start two sleeps at once and wait for both
a: sleep 1 & sleep 1 & wait
ref: wait
teach: wait
---
q: wait for one specific background job by its pid
a: sleep 1 & wait $!
ref: wait
teach: wait
---
q: %list% the jobs running in this shell
a: jobs
ref: sh
teach: sh
---
q: run @script@ so it keeps running after the terminal goes away
a: nohup ./@script@ &
ref: nohup
teach: nohup
hint: nohup makes the process ignore the hangup signal
---
q: run @script@ in a brand new session, fully detached from this terminal
a: setsid ./@script@
ref: setsid
teach: setsid
---
q: give @script@ at most 5 seconds to finish, then kill it
a: timeout 5 ./@script@
out: running
ref: timeout
teach: timeout
---
q: give sleep 10 two seconds, then send it KILL rather than TERM
a: timeout -s KILL 2 sleep 10
ref: timeout
teach: timeout -s
---
q: run sleep 10 with a 1 second limit, sending KILL if it ignores the first signal
a: timeout -k 1 1 sleep 10
ref: timeout
teach: timeout -k
---
q: start a detached session called work running a shell, so it survives a drop
a: abduco -n work /bin/sh
ref: abduco
teach: abduco -n
---
q: start a session called work and attach to it now
a: abduco -c work /bin/sh
ref: abduco
teach: abduco -c
---
q: reattach to the session called work
a: abduco -a work
ref: abduco
teach: abduco -a
---
q: attach to work if it exists, create it if it does not
a: abduco -A work /bin/sh
ref: abduco
teach: abduco -A
---
q: watch the session work without being able to type into it
a: abduco -r work
ref: abduco
teach: abduco -r
---
q: %list% the sessions abduco is holding
a: abduco
ref: abduco
teach: abduco
---
q: attach to work using a different detach key, ctrl-q
a: abduco -e ^q -a work
ref: abduco
teach: abduco -e
---
q: %list% the jobs in this shell with their process ids
a: sleep 1 & jobs -l
ref: jobs
teach: jobs -l
---
q: send a hangup signal to process 12345 by number
a: kill -1 12345
ref: kill
teach: kill -1
---
q: %show% every resource limit this shell is under
a: ulimit -a
out: .
ref: ulimit
teach: ulimit -a
---
q: %show% the maximum file size this shell may create
a: ulimit -f
out: .
ref: ulimit
teach: ulimit -f
